WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.456
One of the most exciting developments
in deep learning has been

2
00:00:07.456 --> 00:00:11.570
the transformer Network, or
sometimes called Transformers.

3
00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:16.570
This is an architecture that has
completely taken the NLP world by storm.

4
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.952
And many of the most effective algorithms for

5
00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:24.541
NLP today are based on
the transformer architecture.

6
00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:29.498
It is a relatively complex neural
network architecture, but in this and

7
00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:34.059
the next three videos after this
will go through it piece by piece.

8
00:00:34.059 --> 00:00:38.789
So that by the end of this next four
videos, you have a good sense of how

9
00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:43.861
the transformer Network works and
you'll be able to apply to your problems.

10
00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:48.085
As the complexity of your
sequence task increases,

11
00:00:48.085 --> 00:00:51.540
so does the complexity of your model.

12
00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.682
We have started this
course with the RNN and

13
00:00:54.682 --> 00:00:59.046
found that it had some problems
with vanishing gradients,

14
00:00:59.046 --> 00:01:04.220
which made it hard to capture long
range dependencies and sequences.

15
00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:10.119
We then looked at the GRU and then
the LSTM model as a way to resolve many

16
00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:17.640
of those problems where you make use of
gates to control the flow of information.

17
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.151
And so each of these units
had a few more computations.

18
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.706
While these editions improved control
over the flow of information,

19
00:01:26.706 --> 00:01:30.340
they also came with increased complexity.

20
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:39.240
So as we move from our RNNs to GRU to
LSTM ,the models became more complex.

21
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.083
And all of these models
are still sequential models in

22
00:01:44.083 --> 00:01:46.880
that they ingested the input,

23
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.640
maybe the input sentence one word or
one token at the time.

24
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:58.265
And so, as as if each unit was like
a bottleneck to the flow of information.

25
00:01:58.265 --> 00:02:03.017
Because to compute the output
of this final unit, for example,

26
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:08.490
you first have to compute the outputs
of all of the units that come before.

27
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:13.634
In this video, you learned about the
transformer architecture, which allows you

28
00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:18.278
to run a lot more of these computations
for an entire sequence in parallel.

29
00:02:18.278 --> 00:02:22.105
So you can ingest an entire
sentence all at the same time,

30
00:02:22.105 --> 00:02:26.671
rather than just processing it one
word at a time from left to right.

31
00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:31.451
The Transformer Network was published
in a seminal paper by Ashish Vaswani

32
00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:36.233
, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar,
Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones,

33
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:39.215
Aidan Gomez, Lukasz Kaiser and Illia Polosukhin.

34
00:02:39.215 --> 00:02:44.302
One of the inventors of
the Transformer network, Lukasz Kaiser,

35
00:02:44.302 --> 00:02:50.140
is also co instructor of the NLP
specialization with deep learning dot AI.

36
00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.739
So you can check that out as well when
you're done with this deep learning

37
00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:54.700
specialization.

38
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:59.740
The major innovation of
the transformer architecture is

39
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:04.886
combining the use of attention
based representations and

40
00:03:04.886 --> 00:03:10.200
a CNN convolutional neural
network style of processing.

41
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.046
So an RNN may process
one output at the time,

42
00:03:16.046 --> 00:03:24.578
and so maybe y(0) feeds in to
them that you compute y(1) and

43
00:03:24.578 --> 00:03:30.240
then this is used to compute y(2).

44
00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.023
This is a very sequential
way of processing tokens,

45
00:03:35.023 --> 00:03:38.610
and you might contrast this with a CNN or

46
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:43.340
confident that can take
input a lot of pixels.

47
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.892
Yeah, or maybe a lot of words and

48
00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:52.692
can compute representations for
them in parallel.

49
00:03:52.692 --> 00:03:58.256
So what you see in the Attention Network
is a way of computing very rich,

50
00:03:58.256 --> 00:04:01.433
very useful representations of words.

51
00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:07.980
But with something more akin to this
CNN style of parallel processing.

52
00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.263
To understand the attention network,

53
00:04:11.263 --> 00:04:16.750
there will be two key ideas will
go through in the next few videos.

54
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.730
The first is self attention.

55
00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.594
The goal of self attention is,
if you have, say,

56
00:04:23.594 --> 00:04:29.114
a sentence of five words will end up
computing five representations for

57
00:04:29.114 --> 00:04:34.365
these five words,
was going to write A1,A2,A3, A4 and A5.

58
00:04:34.365 --> 00:04:40.327
And this will be an attention based
way of computing representations for

59
00:04:40.327 --> 00:04:43.950
all the words in your
sentence in parallel.

60
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:50.900
Then multi headed attention is basically for
loop over the self attention process.

61
00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:55.388
So you end up with multiple
versions of these representations.

62
00:04:55.388 --> 00:04:58.646
And it turns out that
these representations,

63
00:04:58.646 --> 00:05:02.655
which will be very rich representations,
can be used for

64
00:05:02.655 --> 00:05:07.270
machine translation or
other NLP tasks to great effectiveness.

65
00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.643
So in the next video, let's jump
in to learn about self attention,

66
00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:14.350
to compute these rich representations.

67
00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.550
The video after that,
we'll talk about multi headed attention.

68
00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.878
And then the final video on transforming
networks will put all of these together so

69
00:05:22.878 --> 00:05:27.040
that you understand how the entire
transformer architecture works into end.

